<v Speaker 1>taken all the way, Yan Rouse 3, Marquita Mahara 2 Quarterfinal 4. Shuhei Nakamura versus Paula Visa Dama De Rosa. There was little doubt as to the big clash thrown up by the quarterfinal lineup. Nakamura against PV DDR was always likely to be a worthy match up and facing off under the Hollywood lights simply added to the drama. Nakamura, already a Grand Prix winner this season, took charge with</v>

00:30:43.460 --> 00:31:14.590
<v Speaker 1>blistering game one start that rendered utterly ineffectual the apparent MVP of the Fairy Stag. Bitter Blossom. A solitary counterspell was the only player of substance from the Brazilian and even he looked a little shell shocked at the brutality of his first game beating after side boarding. It was Nakamura who had the Bitterblossom threat in Game 2 with a pair of the much vaunted Black Enchantments. Paulo made a slow start, but threatened plenty of action late with back-to-back Ancestral Visions.</v>

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:45.000
<v Speaker 1>A combination of Damnation and Razormane Masticore brought Palo back into the game, but the inevitable Fairy Army began to do their work again for Nakamura. Still, this came at a cost, and despite being apparently on offence, Nakamura was running out of time before he would die. The death of 1000 cuts via double Bitterblossom. Suddenly, stabilization was all Palo needed, and a Spell Stutter Sprite countered a terror that might have gained Nakamura a couple more crucial turns.</v>

00:31:45.180 --> 00:32:17.470
<v Speaker 1>The clock ran out, and it was one one. Game 3 was once again all Nakamura. A crucial Thoughtseize set the tone, stealing away Palio's Bitterblossom and allowing Nakamura the edge with Tarmogoyf and the Three three Death Tarch Wren's run Vanquisher. In the right situation, Murderous Redcap can be good times, but this was not it, and even the mighty cryptic Commands Paulo was holding turned into little more than swimming against the Tide fog effects, one manner shy of getting Razormane Masticore into the action.</v>

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:48.120
<v Speaker 1>Harlow continued to lose ground and Bitterblossom on the Japanese side ended things, moving Nakamura 2 up with two to play. Well, that's two to play if Paula could take it that far. Of course, both players had less than spectacular openings to Game 4, but that suited Palo perfectly, as he was finally able to cast Razormane Masticore when it looked as if it could really dominate the game. The Brazilian was determined to back his artifact to the hilt, determining that it was time to get things done at top speed,</v>

00:32:48.200 --> 00:33:18.860
<v Speaker 1>throwing caution to the winds and simply smashing face. Turns out miss buying Cleek is quite useful at winning races, and also kind of handy it Yes, smashing face. 35 minutes in and these two greatly respected and respectful. Prose had drawn level at 2 each and now faced a single dual shootout. Generally, a turn 2 Bitter Blossom is pretty good news for a fairies deck in game five of a crunch match against one of the best in the game playing in his fifth Pro Tour</v>

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:48.630
<v Speaker 1>top 8, it should be pretty golden for Palo. It wasn't. Shuhei simply murderize him over 3 unforgettable turns. Turn 1, land into Llanowar Elf. Turn 2, land Renz run Vanquisher, a second Llanowar Elf. Turn 3 and this is the big one. Garrick Wild Speaker, add a counter untapped 2 land Bitter Blossom. Ohh, my Bitterblossom not looking quite so good now.</v>

00:33:48.950 --> 00:34:11.820
<v Speaker 1>From that point on, pallet kept more or less level, but he couldn't do anything to rectify the situation. Nakamura, not known for costly errors, methodically took advantage of his astonishing start, and in a match as friendly as it was speedy, Paulo smiled and bowed out of the contest. Shuhei Nakamura 3. Paula Vita Damada Rosa 2.</v>

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:45.740
<v Speaker 1>Yan Russ versus Shuhei Nakamura never want to take an opponent lightly. Shuhei Nakamura must have felt some semblance of a job well done, having vanquished Palomita Dalmida Rosa in the quarters and now facing a less storied opponent in the semis. Ruse, meanwhile, underneath a calm exterior, was having the time of his life and was determined to enjoy the ride for as long as it lasted. Game one saw plenty of red zone</v>

00:34:45.810 --> 00:35:15.830
<v Speaker 1>function as the fundamentally bigger and nastier elves look to gain an edge before Lord of Atlantis and Merrow Reejerey made Russia's army take a trip to the gym. Crucial to this first game was Sygg River Guide, who consistently worked brilliantly for Roose on offence and defence in the red zone. He could squeeze his fellow merfolk through apparently invisible gaps in the Japanese defence and on the backswing could protect his critical teammates from death by nameless inversion, profane command</v>

00:35:15.950 --> 00:35:45.880
<v Speaker 1>and after sideboard wing terror. Even Nakamura's Garruk didn't survive long thanks to Sig. Nonetheless, Nakamura's deck was packed with good stuff and he was steadily developing a very nasty army. With Russ out of gas, he needed something good from the top of his library, and it doesn't get much better than Cryptic Command that allowed him to attack with everything and then change the math totally on the backswing. A second Lord of Atlantis took the game for the German, giving the underdog A1 nil lead.</v>

00:35:46.770 --> 00:36:17.660
<v Speaker 1>That one nil lead quickly doubled, with Nakamura unable to deal with a card out of Russ' sideboard revloc. With the dreadful threat of returning not one, but 2 Lords to play if it ever left the scene, the Reveillark represented a monumental threat to the Japanese plans. When Russ turned it sideways for four damage, Shoe Hayes sent something very bad coming right around the corner, and he wasn't wrong. Cryptic Command not only tapped his entire team, but also bounced Reveillark back to Russ's hand,</v>

00:36:17.750 --> 00:36:48.150
<v Speaker 1>triggering the very thing Nakamura needed to avoid, the return of the Lords. With no comeback from there, Shuhei was forced to move to game three, looking down the barrel of a series sweep. However, this was Shoe Haze fifth Top 8 appearance, and without a Pro Tour win to his name, he wasn't simply going to pack up and go home. He came roaring back in game three with a quick start that led to Imperious Perfect threatening to ruin Russ's day. So Are of Temptation took the Perfect</v>

00:36:48.240 --> 00:37:08.240
<v Speaker 1>day, but only briefly as Shuhei was holding Profane Command and he spent no time hanging around getting back his monster monster and returning Wren's run Vanquisher to play undoing Rouses earlier Sunlance Silver Giller Damped brought Russ no lifeline off the top and Nakamura had closed the gap to 21.</v>

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:39.550
<v Speaker 1>Game 4 began with Sig River Guide again doing sterling work for the German, with Tideshaper Mystic also generously donating an island to Nakamura. Russ was able to pile in with island walking damage and Sig meant that a slow hand with large amounts of removal was getting the Japanese pro precisely nowhere. His only hope was to race. I hope that might have been enhanced by the appearance of Garruk Wildspeaker were it not for the fact that Russ was able to simply Ireland walk his way through</v>

00:37:39.650 --> 00:38:09.840
<v Speaker 1>then to end the planeswalker. With the German relentlessly applying the pressure and crucially making no mistakes to let Nakamura back in, there was nothing Shuhei could do, and the German ruse had made it past an outstanding opponent to reach the Pro Tour final. Yan Rouse 3 Shuhei Nakamura 1 Semifinal 2 Charles Gindy versus Yong Hand Chew Although not quite so comprehensive a beating</v>

00:38:09.910 --> 00:38:40.150
<v Speaker 1>as the quarterfinal, Chew once again found himself the match up favorite with his Reveillark deck against Gindes Green Black Elves. Things didn't start out that way though, as Tues deck properly malfunctioned, throwing up a double Mulligan that was compounded by turn 1 Thoughtseize from Guindy. Although a combination of Chews, Faerie Conclave and his own Llanowar Wastes dropped Gindy to single digits, his own army was steadily growing to lethal proportions off the back of Imperious Perfect, and when Chewing</v>

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:50.450
<v Speaker 1>wanted to steal it with the ubiquitous Sower of Temptation, Gindy had removal to take the Elf straight back, and the double Mulligan had proved too much to overcome.</v>

00:38:51.320 --> 00:39:21.410
<v Speaker 1>Both players kicked off with six. In the second, Gindy looked to have an early lead with Double Thoughtseize, which got rid of Careful Consideration and subsequently Reveillark from Choose Options list. Chameleon Colossus threatened to give the American A2 Nil lead, but this time Tuesday cooperated, turning up a Revloc to sit prettily in play right behind the suspended Greater Gargadon. One turn later, we saw just how powerful that was, as the Revelant blocked stacked damage</v>

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:51.630
<v Speaker 1>sacrificed to the Gargadon and brought back Sower of Temptation to steal an American Imperious Perfect Plus Mulldrifter to draw 2 cards. Outrageous Score Line might still have given Gindy a chance, but then Sir delayed that by a crucial turn and Chew was back on track at 11. Now, as I said in the preview to the Top 8 on Super Sunday, there was a fair bet that you'd never actually get to see in the Reveillark combo in all its glory. Because half the time</v>

00:39:51.720 --> 00:40:21.510
<v Speaker 1>opponents just swept up their permanence and got on with the next game rather than suffer the agony of watching all their permanence being bounced, their opponent gaining theoretically infinite life or just drawing most of their deck before winning Leisure. While that was the story of Game 3, Gindy had nothing doing for three first crucial turns, and that was more than enough of a slow getaway to allow Chew to establish the killer combo. Body double in play. Revloc in the graveyard.</v>

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:51.900
<v Speaker 1>Suspended Gargadon, that was that, and Gindi fell behind for the first time in the top 8. Given that she was the favourites, and given that he was ahead, and given that Gindy had another sluggish start to game four, it's surprising to report that we even got to Game 5. But we did, with Gindy finally making use of a Primal command, sending to Fairies mode, packing and utilizing the little used but rather good against Reveillark, actually shuffle Graveyard into Library ability.</v>

00:40:52.360 --> 00:41:22.590
<v Speaker 1>Multiple treats of villages allowed Chew little time to recover, and it was off to the decider. Even now, with an Anything can Happen jewel ahead, Gindy knew he was the underdog. Although the entire top eight had been played in an atmosphere of conviviality, goodwill and sportsmanship, Kindly allowed himself the grinning luxury of chanting for mulligans from 2, though, presumably coincidentally, Choose Deck obliged, first dropping to six and then five cards.</v>

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:53.020
<v Speaker 1>Well, that was Part 1 of what Gindy needed. Part 2 came with his own opening hand. Turn 2 saw Llanowar Elves into play. That meant the absolute backbreaking Chameleon Colossus. As early as turn three against an opponent desperately searching for land, only a suspended Gargadon came from the Singapore side, and that left the door wide open. Gindy, who's been playing Magic for over half is 23 years, saw his opportunity and kicked the door down savagely</v>

00:41:53.100 --> 00:42:23.120
<v Speaker 1>with the Turn 4 Garruk Wildspeaker backed up by Boreal Druid. With still nothing to do, Chu could only watch as the mighty Garag gave everything plus 3 + 3 and trample and ended the game on turn 5. Chew didn't really know how Gindy had done it. Gindy really didn't quite know how Gindy had done it. But done it he had, and now the German Jan Ruse would await him in the showdown for $40,000. Charles Gindy, 3, Young Hand,</v>

